  "nameStory": "The Quebec Sweat Bee gets its name from being first scientifically described from specimens collected in Quebec, Canada. Like other sweat bees, it's called a 'sweat bee' because these small bees are often attracted to the salt in human perspiration on hot summer days. You might notice one of these tiny visitors landing on your arm during garden work!",
  "description": "The Quebec Sweat Bee is a small, dark-colored native bee that's part of the diverse sweat bee family. These bees are typically black or dark brown with a somewhat metallic sheen, and measure only about a quarter to half-inch long. They're solitary ground-nesting bees, meaning each female digs her own burrow rather than living in a hive. Despite their small size, they're important pollinators of wildflowers and garden plants throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Size and Build",
      "description": "Very small bee, about the size of your pinky nail, with a compact, robust body that feels substantial despite its tiny size",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Coloration",
      "description": "Dark blackish-brown body with a subtle metallic sheen that catches the light like polished gunmetal",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Wing Appearance",
      "description": "Clear to slightly smoky wings that appear proportionally large for the body, with visible dark veins",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Behavior",
      "description": "Often seen flying close to bare soil or visiting small flowers; may land on sweaty skin and walk around calmly",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flight Pattern",
      "description": "Quick, darting flight with frequent hovering near ground level, especially around potential nesting sites",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Quebec Sweat Bees prefer areas with exposed, well-drained soil for nesting, including garden beds, trails, and sunny slopes. They're often found in mixed habitats where wildflowers and garden plants provide nectar sources near suitable nesting ground. Look for them in suburban yards, park edges, and woodland clearings throughout New England's lower elevations.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Adults emerge from underground chambers and begin foraging on early wildflowers. Females start excavating new nest burrows in suitable soil.",
    "summer": "Peak activity period when you're most likely to spot them visiting flowers or hovering near nesting areas. This is when they might land on sweaty skin.",
    "fall": "Late-season individuals may still be active on warm days, visiting fall-blooming flowers like asters before preparing for winter dormancy.",
    "winter": "Inactive, overwintering as adults in underground chambers or as developed larvae in nest cells, waiting for spring warmth."
  },
  "funFact": "Quebec Sweat Bees are such efficient pollinators that a single female can visit over 1,000 flowers in just one day, carrying pollen grains that are nearly as big as her head back to provision each tiny underground nest cell!",
  "confusionNotes": "Easily confused with other small dark sweat bees in the same genus - the Lasioglossum group contains over 40 species in New England! Look for the specific combination of size, dark coloration with metallic hints, and ground-nesting behavior, though definitive identification often requires expert examination.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The genus name Lasioglossum means 'hairy tongue' in Greek, referring to the fuzzy mouthparts these bees use to lap up nectar. Despite being called 'sweat bees,' they're not aggressive and rarely sting - they're simply attracted to the minerals in perspiration.",
  "compareHints": "Among its many Lasioglossum relatives, Quebec Sweat Bee lacks the bright metallic green or blue coloration of species like the Deep-blue Sweat Bee, and is generally darker than the White-banded Sweat Bee. It's distinguished from the Leathery Sweat Bee by its smaller size and less pronounced surface texture, though microscopic examination is often needed for certain identification within this challenging genus.",
